<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TActiveFileUpload</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveFileUpload" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActiveFileUpload</tt> displays a file upload field on a page. Upon postback,
the text entered into the field will be treated as the name of the file
that will be uploaded to the server. The property <tt>HasFile</tt>
indicates whether the file upload is successful. If successful, the file
may be obtained by calling<tt>saveAs</tt> to save it at a specified place.
You can use <tt>FileName</tt>, <tt>FileType</tt>,
<tt>FileSize</tt> to get the original client-side file name,
the file mime type, and the file size information. If the upload is not
successful, <tt>ErrorCode</tt> contains the error code
describing the cause of failure.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
TActiveFileUpload raises <tt>OnFileUpload</tt> event if a file is uploaded
(whether it succeeds or not).
</p>

<p class="block-content">
TActiveFileUpload actually does a postback in a hidden IFrame, and then does a callback.
This callback then raises the <tt>OnFileUpload</tt> event. After the postback
a status icon is displayed; either a green checkmark if the upload is successful,
or a red x if there was an error.
</p>


<h2>Properties</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TempPath</tt></td>
	<td>Where the uploaded file is held until the handler saves it. It is written in namespace form and defaults to <tt>Application.runtime.*</tt>. The directory must be writable by the web server.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>BusyImage</tt></td>
	<td>The image shown while the transfer runs.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>SuccessImage</tt></td>
	<td>The image shown when the transfer finished, a green check by default.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>ErrorImage</tt></td>
	<td>The image shown when the transfer failed, a red cross by default.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>CallbackJavascript</tt></td>
	<td>Read-only. The script that starts the upload. A page with <tt>AutoPostBack</tt> off calls it from its own control, such as a button, to begin the transfer.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
All three images are <tt>TImage</tt> controls, so their subproperties set the source and
the alternate text.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TActiveFileUpload ID="Upload"
    TempPath="Application.runtime.uploads"
    BusyImage.ImageUrl="spinner.gif"
    OnFileUpload="fileUploaded" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The multiple file upload of <a href="?page=Controls.FileUpload">TFileUpload</a> applies
here too, so <tt>Multiple</tt>, <tt>Files</tt>, and <tt>HasAllFiles</tt> work the same way.
</p>

